================================================================================
TEST T33247 -- IPv4_ARP - Clear ARP
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-15 18:33-18:42 NZDT (the DUT's clock reads UTC and runs 3 s behind
          the box's, measured by bracketing `show clock` between two box readings)
Bench:    tb277, consoles u2 = swi_d (DUT); Test Engineer bidhanc@bidhanc-dl
Tester:   bench-runner
Graded:   tester
Case:     T33247 "IPv4_ARP - Clear ARP". No authored step text exists for this case, in
          AWPTCM or in ck.db: an exact-title lookup found step text for one other case
          in this campaign and none for this one, so there are no authored steps and no
          authored expected results. The headings below say what was actually
          exercised. The case was graded against:
            - X960 platform support TPS row 19 "Clear ARP", priority 1, "Yes", note
              column empty;
            - the IP Feature Overview and Configuration Guide, "deleting ARP entries";
            - the DUT's own CLI surface, read off by a read-only `?` probe (quoted in
              STEP 0).
          Objective: all three implemented forms of `clear arp-cache` remove the dynamic
          entries they name and no others; the removal is real in the DATAPLANE, not
          merely in the table read-back; a statically configured entry is NOT removed by
          any of them; and the cache repopulates normally afterwards. 5 steps as
          executed, plus a baseline.
Build:    swi_d  sin_x96056-20261012-24  (flash:/x960-tb277.rel, build date Mon Oct 12 2026)
          AT-x960-56YSQ, S/N A2WQS700B, standalone, console u2 @115200
Configs:  swi_d.cfg (running-config after setup, before step 1; saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- all three implemented forms of `clear arp-cache` clear exactly the
dynamic entries they name and no others, proven in the dataplane and not only in
`show arp`; a statically configured entry is explicitly protected and still forwards
after every clear; and the cache repopulates normally afterwards.
      cache populated and in use (baseline)  10 of 10 frames forwarded, each rewritten
                                             to its entry's MAC, zero ARP requests
      `clear arp-cache A.B.C.D` ............ named entry gone: 3 broadcast ARP requests
                                             and 0 of 5 frames forwarded for it
      ... and only that entry .............. control in the same leg still 5 of 5; the
                                             surviving entry proven on the wire by the
                                             DUT's own unicast ICMP unreachables
      `clear arp-cache` (unqualified) ...... BOTH dynamic entries gone; no unreachables
                                             this time, the DUT broadcasts for the
                                             second address itself
      `clear arp-cache global` ............. the dynamic entry in the global table gone,
                                             the static entry untouched
      `clear arp-cache <static address>` ... refused: "% Skipping 0011.2233.4499 as it
                                             is statically assigned."
      static entry after every clear ....... still 5 of 5 forwarded to 00:11:22:33:44:99
      repopulation ......................... re-learned at the first attempt after each
                                             clear, no configuration change needed
      `clear arp-cache vrf WORD` ........... NOT TESTED (see CAVEAT)
CAVEAT:  The `vrf` form of the command was NOT TESTED: no VRF exists on the DUT in this
         configuration, so there was nothing for it to act on. `clear arp-cache` is an
         EXEC command with no configuration footprint, so there is no running-config
         line to remove and nothing to carry across a reboot; the two equivalent
         properties are covered instead by STEP 5 (the effect is an event, not a sticky
         state). Two dynamic entries and one static entry, IPv4 only, one unit.

