  Method: nothing rests on `show arp`. Each clear is followed by a crafted leg that
  counts what crosses the DUT on the far wire, with an entry that must SURVIVE in the
  same leg as the control. Populating the cache needed its own measurement first: on
  this build a dynamic entry is created only for a neighbour that ANSWERS an ARP request
  the DUT ITSELF sent. Two legs sent `who-has 192.168.10.1 tell 192.168.10.2` from eth3
  and `who-has 192.168.48.1 tell 192.168.48.2` from eth4; the DUT answered both
  (`ARP-REPLY 192.168.10.1 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43` and `ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.1 is-at
  e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43`), yet `show arp` afterwards held only the static entry and NEITHER
  sender was learned. Being asked is not enough. Every leg that needs a dynamic entry
  therefore runs arpleg.py's background responder, which answers the DUT's own request.

STEP 0 -- baseline: the cache is populated, correct and in use              done
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The CLI surface first, read-only, with no command executed:

awplus# clear arp-cache ?
  A.B.C.D  Internet Protocol (IP)
  global   Global Routing/Forwarding table
  vrf      VRF instance
  <cr>
awplus# clear arp-cache 192.168.10.2 ?
  <cr>
                                         <-- the command is `clear arp-cache`; `clear
                                             arp` on this build takes only `security`

  The static control entry is added before the first step, and the dynamic cache is
  emptied so the case starts from a known state:

awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# arp 192.168.48.9 0011.2233.4499 port1.0.48
awplus(config)# end
awplus# clear arp-cache

  Both dynamic entries are then learned through the responder (leg `L0c-learn` from
  eth3 and leg `L0d-learn10` from eth4, each one request and one reply):

  sudo python3.8 tools/arpleg.py --tag L0c-learn --out <capture dir> \
    --iface eth3 --watch eth4 --probe 192.168.48.2 --sender-ip 192.168.10.2 \
    --sender-mac 00:11:22:33:44:01 --gw-mac e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 --count 5 \
    --answer 192.168.48.2=00:11:22:33:44:02,192.168.10.2=00:11:22:33:44:01 \
    --answer-iface eth3,eth4 --wait 6
answering ARP for: 192.168.10.2=00:11:22:33:44:01, 192.168.48.2=00:11:22:33:44:02 on eth3,eth4
answerer sent 1 ARP reply/replies (eth3:0, eth4:1)
cap L0c-learn-eth4.pcap: 7 frame(s)
  x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.2 :9 [ours]  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]
  x1  ARP-REQ   who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:02]
  x1  ARP-REPLY 192.168.48.2 is-at 00:11:22:33:44:02    [00:11:22:33:44:02 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]

  The state the clears must demonstrably destroy:

