  => STEP 0 done: 10 of 10 frames forwarded, each rewritten to the MAC its entry holds,
     and not one ARP request on either wire. The cache is populated, correct and in use.
     This is what every clear below is measured against.

STEP 1 -- `clear arp-cache A.B.C.D` clears exactly the entry named         PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# clear arp-cache 192.168.48.2
awplus# show arp
IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
192.168.10.2    0011.2233.4401   vlan10               port1.0.10  dynamic
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static
                                         <-- 192.168.48.2 gone; the other dynamic entry
                                             and the static one remain

  leg `L2-single-clear`, the same invocation as the baseline leg, no responder:
LEG L2-single-clear  sent total: 10 frame(s)
  cap L2-single-clear-eth3.pcap: 15 frame(s)
    x5  UDP  192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.2 :9 [ours]  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x5  UDP  192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.9 :9 [ours]  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x5  ICMP t3/c1 192.168.10.1 -> 192.168.10.2 [ours] [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:01]
                                         <-- UNICAST unreachables: only a surviving entry
                                             could address these
  cap L2-single-clear-eth4.pcap: 8 frame(s)
    x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.9 :9 [ours]  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:99]
    x3  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]

  Three independent facts in one leg:
    - CLEARED: 192.168.48.2 is now unresolved. The DUT broadcast 3 ARP requests for it
      and forwarded 0 of its 5 frames -- against 5 of 5 with no ARP at all in STEP 0.
    - NOT CLEARED, control in the same leg: 192.168.48.9 still forwarded 5 of 5 to
      00:11:22:33:44:99.
    - NOT CLEARED, proven on the wire rather than from the table: the DUT returned 5
      ICMP type 3 code 1 (destination host unreachable) to 192.168.10.2, addressed
      UNICAST to 00:11:22:33:44:01. It could only address those because its
      192.168.10.2 entry had survived. That is the dataplane confirming what `show arp`
      claimed.

  => STEP 1 PASS: the single-address form clears the entry it names and leaves the
     others in place, and both halves of that are measured on the wire -- the cleared
     address is re-ARPed for and stops forwarding, while the DUT's own unicast ICMP
     proves the untouched dynamic entry is still installed.

STEP 2 -- `clear arp-cache` with no argument clears ALL dynamic entries and no static one  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  192.168.48.2 was first re-learned (leg `L2b-repop`, responder sent 1 reply), so that
  two dynamic entries existed to be cleared.

awplus# show arp
192.168.10.2    0011.2233.4401   vlan10               port1.0.10  dynamic
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static
192.168.48.2    0011.2233.4402   vlan48               port1.0.48  dynamic
awplus# clear arp-cache
awplus# show arp
IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static
                                         <-- BOTH dynamic entries gone, the static one
                                             untouched

  leg `L3-full-clear`, same invocation again:
LEG L3-full-clear  sent total: 10 frame(s)
  cap L3-full-clear-eth3.pcap: 13 frame(s)
    x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.2 :9 [ours]  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.9 :9 [ours]  [00:11:22:33:44:01 -> e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43]
    x3  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.10.2 tell 192.168.10.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
                                         <-- no unreachables now; the DUT must broadcast
                                             for 192.168.10.2 itself
  cap L3-full-clear-eth4.pcap: 8 frame(s)
    x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.9 :9 [ours]  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:99]
    x3  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]

  This is the discriminator that separates this step from STEP 1. There, the DUT
  answered the unresolvable traffic with ICMP unreachables addressed to 192.168.10.2,
  because that entry had survived. After the unqualified clear there are NO ICMP
  unreachables; instead the DUT has to BROADCAST 3 ARP requests for 192.168.10.2 itself.
  The static entry still forwarded 5 of 5.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the unqualified form clears every dynamic entry and no static one, and
     the two clear forms are distinguishable on the wire -- by the presence or absence
     of the DUT's own unicast unreachables -- not only in the table.

STEP 3 -- `clear arp-cache global`                                          PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  192.168.48.2 was re-learned again first (leg `L4a-repop`, responder sent 1 reply).

awplus# show arp
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static
192.168.48.2    0011.2233.4402   vlan48               port1.0.48  dynamic
awplus# clear arp-cache global
awplus# show arp
IP Address      LL Address       Interface            Port        Type
192.168.48.9    0011.2233.4499   vlan48               port1.0.48  static

  The dynamic entry in the global routing/forwarding table is cleared and the static
  entry is not -- the same outcome as the unqualified form on a device with no VRFs
  configured, which is what "global" distinguishes itself from here.

  => STEP 3 PASS for the `global` form: it clears the dynamic entries of the global
     routing/forwarding table and protects the static entry. The `vrf WORD` form is
     NOT TESTED -- no VRF exists on the DUT in this configuration, so the form had
     nothing to act on; it is recorded rather than claimed.

STEP 4 -- a static entry cannot be cleared, and the DUT says so             PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# clear arp-cache 192.168.48.9
% Skipping 0011.2233.4499 as it is statically assigned.
% Can't find address
awplus# show running-config | include arp
arp 192.168.48.9 0011.2233.4499 port1.0.48

  The static entry is explicitly protected, with a message that names the MAC and the
  reason. The second line follows because, the static entry having been skipped, no
  clearable entry for that address remained. Both lines are the expected result of a
  negative step. The configuration line is untouched.

  leg `L5-static-survives`, after a single clear, an unqualified clear, a global clear
  and this attempted clear of the static entry itself:
LEG L5-static-survives  sent total: 10 frame(s)
  cap L5-static-survives-eth4.pcap: 8 frame(s)
    x5  UDP 192.168.10.2 -> 192.168.48.9 :9 [ours]  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 00:11:22:33:44:99]
    x3  ARP-REQ who-has 192.168.48.2 tell 192.168.48.1  [e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]

  => STEP 4 PASS: a static entry survives every form of the command, is refused by name
     with a specific message when cleared directly, and is still in the forwarding path
     afterwards -- 5 of 5 rewritten to 00:11:22:33:44:99 -- while the dynamic address
     cleared alongside it is still unresolved.

STEP 5 -- the cache repopulates after a clear: the clear is an event, not a state  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Demonstrated three times over, after the clears of STEPS 2, 3 and 4: each time a leg
  with the responder running (`L2b-repop`, `L4a-repop`) re-learned 192.168.48.2 at the
  first attempt -- `show arp` listed it as `dynamic` again and the next leg forwarded to
  it with no ARP request at all, as quoted in those steps.

  => STEP 5 PASS: no configuration change was needed to re-enable learning and no clear
     left the cache permanently disabled. The effect of `clear arp-cache` is confined to
     the entries present when it is issued -- which is the property that stands in for a
     removal/persistence check on an EXEC command with no configuration footprint.
